      "text": "The median of a set of numbers is the middle number when the set is arranged in ascending order. The given set is $\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}$, and we are told that the median is 9.\n\nFirst, let's arrange the set in ascending order. The set becomes $\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}$.\n\nSince the set has five numbers, the median is the third number in the ordered list.\n\nTherefore, we will first solve for $n$:\n\n$$n + 6 = 9$$\n$$n = 9 - 6$$\n$$n = 3$$\n\nNow that we have the value of $n$ (which is 3), we can find the mean of the set:\n\n$$\\frac{(n) + (n + 5) + (n + 6) + (n + 9) + (n + 15)}{5}$$\n\nLet's simplify this expression:\n\n$$\\frac{(3) + (3 + 5) + (3 + 6) + (3 + 9) + (3 + 15)}{5}$$\n$$\\frac{3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18}{5}$$\n$$\\frac{50}{5}$$\n$$10$$\n\nHence, the mean of the set is \\boxed{10}.",
