      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the value of \\( n \\) first, using the information that the median of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\) is 9. We then use this value of \\( n \\) to find the mean of the set.\n\nThe median of a set of numbers is the middle number when the set is ordered. For a set with an even number of elements, the median is the average of the two middle numbers. Here, the set has 5 elements, so the median is the third element when ordered.\n\nGiven that the median is 9, the third element \\( n + 6 \\) must be equal to 9. So, we can solve for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nn + 6 = 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\nn = 3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( n = 3 \\), we can substitute this value into the set to get the set \\(\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\\).\n\nThe mean of a set is the sum of the elements divided by the number of elements. The sum of the elements in the set is:\n\n\\[\n3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50\n\\]\n\nSince there are 5 elements, the mean is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\). Let's verify this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the set with n = 3\nn = 3\nelements = [n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15]\n\n# Calculate the median\nmedian = sorted(elements)[2]\n\n# Check if the median is 9\nassert median == 9\n\n# Calculate the mean\nmean = sum(elements) / len(elements)\nprint(mean)\n```",
